LZ.data.lpapers.b1.push({id:"b1l1",title:"Listening Test 1",topics:"Everyday situations · A summer art course · Opinions · A young baker",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You will hear a woman talking to her son.",q:"Where are the car keys?",o:["in her bag","on the kitchen table","in his coat pocket"],a:2,x:"‘Sorry, Mum – I think they're still in my coat pocket.’ Her bag and the kitchen table are the places she has already looked.",script:[["F","Tom, have you seen the car keys? I need to go to the supermarket."],["M","Aren't they in your bag? That's where you usually keep them."],["F","I've looked – they're not there. I was sure I'd left them on the kitchen table, but there's nothing there except the newspaper."],["M","Oh, wait. I borrowed the car yesterday to go to football. Sorry, Mum – I think they're still in my coat pocket. I'll get them."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man talking about his holiday.",q:"What did he enjoy most?",o:["the museums","the beach","the food"],a:2,x:"‘What I'll really remember is the food.’ It was too windy to swim, and the museums were ‘nothing special’.",script:[["M","The hotel was right next to the beach, but to be honest it was too windy to swim most days. We visited a couple of museums when it rained, which were fine, but nothing special. What I'll really remember is the food. Every evening we tried a different little restaurant in the old town, and the fish was amazing. I've even bought a cookbook so I can make some of the dishes at home."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a girl leaving a message for a friend.",q:"What time will they meet?",o:["at 6.30","at 7.30","at 8.00"],a:1,x:"‘Shall we meet at half past seven instead?’ Half past six was the old plan and eight is when the film starts.",script:[["F","Hi Jess, it's Mia. I've just checked the cinema website, and the film doesn't start at seven like we thought – it starts at eight. So we don't need to meet at half past six any more. Shall we meet at half past seven instead? That gives us time to buy some popcorn. Call me back if that's a problem. Bye!"]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man asking about something he lost at a sports centre.",q:"What has the man lost?",o:["a pair of glasses","a watch","a phone"],a:1,x:"‘It's a watch – a black one with a leather strap.’ He has his phone, and the glasses belong to someone else.",script:[["M","Excuse me, I was here for a swim this morning, and I think I left something in the changing room."],["F","What is it? We've had a few phones handed in today."],["M","No, I've got my phone here. It's a watch – a black one with a leather strap. It was a present from my wife."],["F","Let me look. There's a pair of glasses and some keys… ah, yes. Is this it?"],["M","That's it! Thank you so much."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a woman talking about how she gets to work.",q:"How does she usually travel to work now?",o:["by bike","by bus","by car"],a:0,x:"‘Now I cycle in almost every day.’ She used to drive and she takes the bus only ‘a few times a month’.",script:[["F","When I started this job, I used to drive, but parking in the city centre is so expensive. Then I tried the bus for a while, but it was always late and really crowded. So last spring I bought a bike, and now I cycle in almost every day. When the weather's really bad I still take the bus, but that's only a few times a month. I feel much healthier."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a teacher talking to a class about a school trip.",q:"What must the students bring?",o:["a packed lunch","some money","a waterproof jacket"],a:2,x:"‘You absolutely must bring a waterproof jacket.’ The tickets are paid for and lunch is provided.",script:[["F","OK, everyone, just a reminder about tomorrow's trip to the castle. You don't need to bring any money – the school has already paid for the tickets. And lunch will be provided in the castle café, so no sandwiches this time. But the forecast says it's going to rain in the afternoon, and we'll be outside a lot, so you absolutely must bring a waterproof jacket. Anyone without one won't be able to come on the walk round the walls."]]},{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a birthday present.",q:"What will they buy for their friend?",o:["a book","a concert ticket","a plant"],a:2,x:"‘Let's get her something green for the living room.’ – ‘A plant! Good idea.’ She has too many books and the tickets are too expensive.",script:[["M","What shall we get Sophie for her birthday? She loves reading – how about a book?"],["F","She's got hundreds of books. And she said she hasn't got space on her shelves."],["M","What about a ticket for that concert next month?"],["F","They're too expensive – and I'm not sure she likes that band any more. She's just moved into her new flat, and she said it needs a bit of colour. Let's get her something green for the living room."],["M","A plant! Good idea. There's a nice shop near the station."]]}]},2:{extracts:[{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a film they have seen.",q:"What does the girl say about the film?",o:["The story was hard to follow.","It was longer than it needed to be.","The actors were disappointing."],a:1,x:"‘Did it need to be three hours? The middle part… could have been half as long.’ The story was ‘easy enough to follow’ and the actors were ‘brilliant’.",script:[["M","So what did you think? I loved it."],["F","Yeah, it was good. The story was easy enough to follow, and the actors were brilliant – especially the girl who played the sister. But honestly, did it need to be three hours? The middle part, with all the travelling, could have been half as long."],["M","Maybe. I didn't notice the time."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a boy telling a friend about a climbing club.",q:"How does the boy feel about the club?",o:["disappointed with the coach","worried that it is too difficult for him","surprised that the members are so helpful"],a:2,x:"He expected people wouldn't want to help a beginner, ‘but it's not like that at all’ – people he'd never met showed him what to do.",script:[["F","How's the climbing club going? You've been three times now, haven't you?"],["M","Yeah. I was really nervous before the first session, because I thought everyone would be much better than me and wouldn't want to help a beginner. But it's not like that at all. People I'd never met before showed me what to do and cheered when I got to the top. The coach is good too, but it's the other members who make it."],["F","That's great."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a woman telling a friend about a cookery class.",q:"Why did she join the class?",o:["to meet new people","to cook better meals for her family","to learn to cook food from another country"],a:2,x:"She ‘fell in love with the food’ in Thailand and signed up for a Thai cookery class. She's ‘not really there to make friends’.",script:[["M","I didn't know you were doing a cookery class."],["F","It's my second month. My family's always said my cooking's fine, and I'm not really there to make friends, although people are nice. It's that I went to Thailand last year and fell in love with the food. So when I saw a Thai cookery class advertised at the college, I signed up immediately. Last week we made a green curry – it was nearly as good as the ones I had in Bangkok!"]]},{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a new shopping centre.",q:"What do they agree about?",o:["It is difficult to get to.","The shops are too expensive.","It is too crowded at weekends."],a:2,x:"She says ‘there were people everywhere’ on Saturday; he ‘could hardly move’ on Sunday and won't go at the weekend again – ‘Me neither.’ They disagree about the prices.",script:[["M","Have you been to the new shopping centre yet?"],["F","Last Saturday. It took ages to find a parking space – there were people everywhere."],["M","Tell me about it. I went on Sunday and I could hardly move. I won't go at the weekend again."],["F","Me neither. The prices are OK, though – not more than in town."],["M","I thought some of the shops were quite expensive, actually. But it's easy to get there – the bus stops right outside."],["F","That's true."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man talking to a friend about his new job.",q:"What does he like best about it?",o:["the people he works with","the hours he works","the money he earns"],a:1,x:"‘The best thing is I finish at three on Fridays, and I start at ten every day.’ The pay isn't much better and he doesn't know his colleagues yet.",script:[["F","How's the new job?"],["M","Really good. The pay isn't much better than at my old company, and I don't really know my colleagues yet – they seem friendly enough. But the best thing is I finish at three on Fridays, and I start at ten every day, so I don't have to get up at six any more. I've got so much more time for myself."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a girl talking to her brother about a concert.",q:"What is she going to do?",o:["watch the concert online","try to get a ticket for another night","go to the concert with her brother"],a:1,x:"An extra concert has been added on Sunday and she's ‘going to set my alarm and try again’. Watching online ‘is not the same’.",script:[["M","Did you get a ticket for Saturday's concert?"],["F","No, they sold out in about five minutes. I'm so annoyed."],["M","They're showing it live online, you know. You could watch it with me."],["F","It's not the same as being there. Anyway, they've just added an extra concert on Sunday. The tickets go on sale tomorrow at nine, so I'm going to set my alarm and try again."],["M","Good luck!"]]}]},3:{title:"Summer Art Course",ctx:"You will hear a woman called Karen Hill talking on the radio about a summer art course for teenagers.",script:[["F",`Hello, and thanks for having me on the programme. I'm Karen Hill, and I run the summer art course for young people aged thirteen to seventeen. This year the course is moving. For the last five years we've been at the college, but this summer we'll be in the old library in the town centre, which has been beautifully repaired and has lots of light – perfect for painting.

The course lasts three weeks, starting on the fourteenth of July, and we meet every weekday morning from ten until one. In the first week, students learn to draw people. It's something many young artists find difficult, so we start with it. In the second week we move on to painting, and in the last week everyone makes something from recycled materials, like old bottles and newspapers.

At the end of the course there's an exhibition of everyone's work in the town hall, and families are welcome to come along. The course costs ninety-five pounds, which includes all materials, but students need to bring their own apron, because it can get quite messy!

To book a place, please don't phone the college. You need to fill in a form on our website, which is young artists dot org. Places are limited to twenty-five students, so book soon.`]],items:[{s:"Place: the {g} in the town centre",a:["old library","library"],x:"‘this summer we'll be in the old library in the town centre’ – the college is where it used to be."},{s:"The course starts on: {g}",a:["14 July","14th July","14th of July","fourteenth of July","the fourteenth of July","July 14","July 14th","the 14th of July","14/7","14.7"],x:"‘starting on the fourteenth of July’"},{s:"Week 1: students learn to draw {g}",a:["people"],x:"‘In the first week, students learn to draw people.’"},{s:"Week 3: students make things from {g} materials",a:["recycled"],x:"‘everyone makes something from recycled materials, like old bottles and newspapers’"},{s:"Students must bring their own {g}",a:["apron","an apron","aprons"],x:"‘students need to bring their own apron’ – materials are included in the price."},{s:"To book, fill in a form on the {g}",a:["website","web site","internet"],x:"‘please don't phone the college. You need to fill in a form on our website’"}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a young woman called Emma Green, who has opened her own bakery.",script:[["M","My guest today is Emma Green, who opened her own bakery last year at the age of just twenty-two. Emma, welcome."],["F","Thanks for inviting me."],["M","When did you first get interested in baking?"],["F","People always expect me to say it was my grandmother who taught me, but actually nobody in my family bakes. When I was about ten, I watched a baking competition on TV and I just thought, I want to try that. My first cake was a disaster, but I kept practising every weekend."],["M","Did you study baking after school?"],["F","I did a course at college for two years. I learnt a lot there, especially about bread, which is much harder than cakes. But the most useful thing was working in a hotel kitchen at the weekends. That's where I learnt to work fast and keep calm when things go wrong."],["M","Why did you decide to open your own bakery?"],["F","I'd always dreamed of it, but I thought I'd wait until I was thirty and had saved some money. Then the little shop on my street became empty, and the rent was surprisingly low. My parents helped me a bit, and I got a small loan from the bank. I thought, if I don't do it now, somebody else will take the shop."],["M","What's a normal day like?"],["F","I get up at four, which my friends think is crazy. The hardest thing isn't the early start, though – I'm used to that now. It's that I'm on my feet for ten hours a day. By the evening my legs really hurt! But I love the moment when I open the door at seven and the first customers come in."],["M","What's your most popular product?"],["F","I thought it would be my chocolate cake, but it's actually a simple cheese and onion roll. The builders working on the new school next door buy about thirty every morning! Cakes sell well at the weekends, when families come in."],["M","And what's next for you?"],["F","Some people have asked me to open a second shop, but I don't want to get too big. What I'd really like is to start baking classes for children on Saturday afternoons, when the shop is quieter. I'd like to give other kids the chance I had."],["M","Emma Green, thank you very much."]],qs:[{q:"What first made Emma interested in baking?",o:["a member of her family","a television programme","a lesson at school"],a:1,x:"‘I watched a baking competition on TV and I just thought, I want to try that.’ ‘Nobody in my family bakes.’"},{q:"What does Emma say was most useful for her?",o:["her college course","her weekend job","advice from a famous baker"],a:1,x:"‘The most useful thing was working in a hotel kitchen at the weekends.’"},{q:"Why did Emma open her bakery earlier than she planned?",o:["A shop in a good place became available.","Her parents offered to pay for everything.","She had saved more money than she expected."],a:0,x:"‘The little shop on my street became empty, and the rent was surprisingly low… if I don't do it now, somebody else will take the shop.’ Her parents only ‘helped a bit’."},{q:"What does Emma find hardest about her job?",o:["getting up early","standing up all day","dealing with customers"],a:1,x:"‘The hardest thing isn't the early start… It's that I'm on my feet for ten hours a day.’"},{q:"What is the most popular product in Emma's bakery?",o:["chocolate cake","a kind of bread roll","biscuits"],a:1,x:"‘I thought it would be my chocolate cake, but it's actually a simple cheese and onion roll.’"},{q:"What does Emma want to do in the future?",o:["open another shop","write a book about baking","teach children to bake"],a:2,x:"‘What I'd really like is to start baking classes for children on Saturday afternoons.’ She doesn't want a second shop."}]}}}),LZ.data.lpapers.b1.push({id:"b1l2",title:"Listening Test 2",topics:"Everyday situations · A wildlife park · Opinions · A cycling trip",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You will hear a man phoning a hotel.",q:"Which room does he want?",o:["a single room","a double room","a family room"],a:1,x:"‘Just a double, please.’ His wife is coming too, so not a single, and the children are staying with their grandparents.",script:[["M","Hello, I'd like to book a room for next Friday and Saturday. It was going to be just me, so I was going to ask for a single, but my wife's decided to come too. Our children are staying with their grandparents, so we won't need a family room. Just a double, please – with a view of the sea, if possible."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a girl talking about her weekend.",q:"What did she do on Saturday?",o:["She went swimming.","She played tennis.","She went shopping."],a:0,x:"‘In the end I went to the swimming pool with my brother.’ Tennis was on Sunday, and they didn't go shopping.",script:[["F","I was supposed to play tennis with Anna on Saturday morning, but it rained all day, so we couldn't. We thought about going shopping instead, but Anna had to help her dad. So in the end I went to the swimming pool with my brother. It was really busy because of the weather, but it was fun. On Sunday it was sunny, so Anna and I finally played tennis."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a woman talking to a shop assistant.",q:"How much will she pay for the T-shirt?",o:["£15","£12","£20"],a:1,x:"‘I can give it to you for twelve pounds’ because it has a small mark. £20 is the normal price and £15 is for the ones by the window.",script:[["F","Excuse me, how much is this T-shirt? There's no price on it."],["M","Those ones are twenty pounds."],["F","Oh. The sign in the window said everything was fifteen pounds."],["M","That's only for the ones on the table by the window. But this one has a small mark on it here, look – so I can give it to you for twelve pounds."],["F","Great, I'll take it."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a boy talking about his new hobby.",q:"What is his new hobby?",o:["taking photos","playing the guitar","painting"],a:0,x:"‘What I've really got into is taking pictures.’ He hasn't touched the guitar much and isn't good at art.",script:[["M","My uncle's a musician, and he gave me his old guitar for my birthday, but to be honest I haven't touched it much. What I've really got into is taking pictures. My mum lent me her old camera, and now I go out every weekend to photograph birds in the park. I'd like to paint them one day, but I'm not very good at art."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a woman leaving a message for a friend.",q:"Where will they meet?",o:["outside the station","in a café","at the museum"],a:1,x:"‘Why don't we meet at the café opposite the museum instead?’ The station is too noisy, and they'll walk to the museum later.",script:[["F","Hi Sarah, it's Kate. About tomorrow – I know we said we'd meet outside the station, but they're doing work on the road there and it's really noisy. Why don't we meet at the café opposite the museum instead? It's called Lemon Tree. We can have a coffee and then walk over to the museum together. See you at ten!"]]},{ctx:"You will hear a weather forecast on the radio.",q:"What will the weather be like tomorrow afternoon?",o:["rainy","sunny","windy"],a:1,x:"‘The afternoon will be bright and sunny.’ The rain is in the morning and the wind ‘will finally drop’.",script:[["M","And now the weather for tomorrow. It'll be a cloudy start in most places, with some heavy rain in the morning, especially in the north. That rain will clear by lunchtime, though, and the afternoon will be bright and sunny, with temperatures reaching twenty-two degrees. The wind that we've had all week will finally drop, so it'll feel quite warm."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a girl talking to her father about a school project.",q:"What does she need help with?",o:["finding information","using the computer","drawing a map"],a:2,x:"‘It's the map of the river that I'm stuck on… You're good at drawing.’ She already has the information and has typed it.",script:[["F","Dad, can you help me with my geography project?"],["M","Sure. Do you need help finding information? I could take you to the library."],["F","No, I've got loads of information from the internet already, and I've typed most of it. It's the map of the river that I'm stuck on. I've tried three times and it looks terrible. You're good at drawing."],["M","OK, let's have a look."]]}]},2:{extracts:[{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a party.",q:"How did the boy feel at the party?",o:["too tired to enjoy it","happy to see old friends","bored because he didn't know anyone"],a:2,x:"He'd have been fine ‘if I'd had someone to talk to’ – everyone was Lucy's cousins and neighbours. He says tiredness ‘wasn't it’.",script:[["F","Did you enjoy Lucy's party on Saturday?"],["M","Honestly, not really. I'd been working all day, but it wasn't that – I'd have been fine if I'd had someone to talk to. I thought some people from our class would be there, but they were all Lucy's cousins and neighbours. I just stood in the kitchen eating crisps."],["F","Oh no!"]]},{ctx:"You will hear a woman telling a friend about a new restaurant.",q:"What does she think about it?",o:["The service was slow.","The food was better than she expected.","It was too expensive."],a:1,x:"She ‘didn't expect much’, but ‘the pizza was one of the best I've ever had’. The waiters were quick and it wasn't expensive.",script:[["M","You went to that new Italian place, didn't you?"],["F","Yes, on Friday. I didn't expect much, because the reviews online weren't great. But the pizza was one of the best I've ever had! The waiters were quick and friendly, and it wasn't expensive at all. I'm definitely going back."]]},{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a TV series.",q:"What does the boy want to do?",o:["watch the next episode with his friend","stop watching the series completely","read the book the series is based on"],a:2,x:"‘I think I'll borrow it from her’ – the book, which his sister says is better. He'd ‘rather not’ watch the next episode together.",script:[["F","Have you seen the latest episode of The Island?"],["M","Yes, but I'm not sure I'll carry on with it. Every episode is the same – someone gets lost, then someone finds them."],["F","It gets better, I promise. Why don't we watch the next one together on Friday?"],["M","Hmm, I'd rather not. Actually, my sister says the book it's based on is much better. I think I'll borrow it from her."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man telling a friend about his new flat.",q:"What does he like most about the flat?",o:["the size of the rooms","the view from the window","the quiet neighbours"],a:1,x:"‘I can see the whole city and the mountains behind it. I never get tired of that.’ The rooms are small and the neighbours play music late.",script:[["F","How's the new flat?"],["M","Great. The rooms aren't very big, to be honest – my sofa only just fits. And the neighbours upstairs play music quite late sometimes. But when I open the curtains in the morning, I can see the whole city and the mountains behind it. I never get tired of that."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a girl telling a friend about learning Spanish.",q:"Why is she learning Spanish?",o:["She needs it for her job.","She is going to live in Spain.","She wants to talk to her boyfriend's family."],a:2,x:"His parents and grandparents ‘don't speak much English’ and she wants ‘to be able to chat to them properly’. She doesn't need it for work.",script:[["M","I didn't know you were learning Spanish."],["F","I started in January. Everyone thinks it's for work, but I don't need it for my job at all. My boyfriend's from Mexico, and his parents and grandparents don't speak much English. We're visiting them in the summer, and I really want to be able to chat to them properly."]]},{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a football match.",q:"What do they agree about?",o:["The team played badly.","The tickets were too expensive.","The weather was terrible."],a:1,x:"She won't pay forty pounds again – ‘You're right. It's far too much.’ They disagree about the weather, and the team played brilliantly.",script:[["M","What a match! Three–nil!"],["F","I know, the team played brilliantly. The weather was awful, though – I was soaked."],["M","Really? I didn't mind the rain. It was worse last week."],["F","Well, anyway, I'm not paying forty pounds for a ticket again. That's crazy."],["M","You're right. It's far too much for a local match."]]}]},3:{title:"Green Valley Wildlife Park",ctx:"You will hear a man called David giving information to visitors at a wildlife park.",script:[["M",`Good morning, everyone, and welcome to Green Valley Wildlife Park. My name's David, and I'm going to tell you about your visit today. The park opened in nineteen ninety-eight, and it now has more than two hundred kinds of animals. You've arrived at a great time, because we've just got two new baby elephants, which were born in March. You'll find them near the lake.

If you want to see the animals being fed, the best time is half past two, when our keepers feed the lions. Please don't feed any of the animals yourselves – some of them can get very ill from human food.

There are three places to eat in the park. The largest is the restaurant by the main gate, but if you want something quick, the café next to the monkey house sells sandwiches and cold drinks. Please stay on the paths at all times.

We have a small train that goes round the whole park. It leaves every twenty minutes from the station beside the gift shop, and it's free for everyone today. Finally, the park closes at six o'clock, but the last entry to the reptile house is at five. Have a lovely day!`]],items:[{s:"The park now has more than {g} kinds of animals.",a:["200","two hundred"],x:"‘it now has more than two hundred kinds of animals’ – 1998 is when it opened."},{s:"The baby elephants can be seen near the {g}.",a:["lake","the lake"],x:"‘You'll find them near the lake.’"},{s:"The keepers feed the lions at {g}.",a:["2.30","2:30","half past two","two thirty","14.30","14:30","2.30 pm","2:30 pm"],x:"‘the best time is half past two, when our keepers feed the lions’"},{s:"For something quick to eat, go to the café next to the {g}.",a:["monkey house"],x:"‘the café next to the monkey house sells sandwiches and cold drinks’"},{s:"The train leaves from beside the {g}.",a:["gift shop"],x:"‘It leaves every twenty minutes from the station beside the gift shop.’"},{s:"The last entry to the reptile house is at {g}.",a:["5","five","five o'clock","5 o'clock","5.00","5:00","5 pm","5pm","17.00","17:00"],x:"‘the last entry to the reptile house is at five’ – six is when the park closes."}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a teenager called Jake Morris, who cycled across Europe with his father.",script:[["F","Today I'm talking to Jake Morris, who spent last summer cycling two thousand kilometres from London to Rome with his dad. Jake, what gave you the idea?"],["M","It was my dad's idea, really. He'd read a book about a man who cycled around the world, and he said, why don't we do something like that? I thought he was joking at first. I'd never cycled further than my school."],["F","How did you prepare?"],["M","We started training in January. Every Saturday we rode a bit further. I thought my legs would be the problem, but actually the hardest part was learning to repair the bikes. Dad made me practise changing tyres in the garden until I could do it in five minutes."],["F","Where did you sleep?"],["M","We took a small tent, and we camped most nights. Sometimes we stayed in cheap hotels when it rained. The best night was in Switzerland, when a farmer let us sleep in his barn and his family gave us dinner."],["F","Were there any bad moments?"],["M","In France, Dad fell off his bike and hurt his arm. We had to stop for four days. I was worried we'd have to go home, but the doctor said it wasn't broken. Actually, those four days were really nice – we explored a little town and I tried snails for the first time!"],["F","What did you learn from the trip?"],["M","I used to spend hours on my phone, and I got bored really easily. On the trip there often wasn't any signal, and I found I didn't miss it. I learnt that I can be happy with very little. And I got to know my dad much better."],["F","What's next?"],["M","Dad wants to cycle to Norway next summer, but I've got exams, so I'll have to say no. Maybe after that. For now, I want to write about the trip for my school website, so other students can see it's possible."],["F","Jake, thanks for talking to us."]],qs:[{q:"Whose idea was the trip?",o:["Jake's","Jake's father's","a writer's"],a:1,x:"‘It was my dad's idea, really.’ The writer only inspired his dad."},{q:"What did Jake find most difficult when he was preparing?",o:["cycling long distances","learning to repair the bikes","finding time to train"],a:1,x:"‘I thought my legs would be the problem, but actually the hardest part was learning to repair the bikes.’"},{q:"Where did Jake spend his best night of the trip?",o:["in a small hotel","in a tent by a lake","on a farm"],a:2,x:"‘The best night was in Switzerland, when a farmer let us sleep in his barn.’"},{q:"How did Jake feel when his father hurt his arm?",o:["angry about losing time","worried that the trip would end","sure that his father would be fine"],a:1,x:"‘I was worried we'd have to go home.’"},{q:"What does Jake say he learned on the trip?",o:["He doesn't need his phone to be happy.","He wants to be a professional cyclist.","He should have trained harder."],a:0,x:"‘There often wasn't any signal, and I found I didn't miss it. I learnt that I can be happy with very little.’"},{q:"What is Jake planning to do next?",o:["cycle to Norway","write about the trip","take part in a race"],a:1,x:"‘For now, I want to write about the trip for my school website.’ He has to say no to Norway because of exams."}]}}}),LZ.data.lpapers.b1.push({id:"b1l3",title:"Listening Test 3",topics:"Everyday situations · A summer festival · Opinions · A young film-maker",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You will hear a boy talking to his mother.",q:"What does he want for his birthday?",o:["a bike","a phone","a guitar"],a:2,x:"‘What I'd really love is a guitar.’ His bike is fine now, and his phone still works.",script:[["M","Mum, about my birthday – I know I said I wanted a new bike, but mine's actually fine now that Dad's fixed it."],["F","What about a phone? Yours is really old."],["M","It still works. What I'd really love is a guitar. Jake's teaching me, and I have to borrow his every time."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a woman talking about her new flat.",q:"Which room does she like best?",o:["the kitchen","the living room","the bedroom"],a:1,x:"‘The living room is lovely… I spend most of my time in there.’ The kitchen is tiny and the bedroom is noisy.",script:[["F","The kitchen's tiny – I can hardly turn round in it. The bedroom's OK, but it faces the road, so it's a bit noisy. But the living room is lovely. It gets the sun all afternoon, and there's a big window looking over the park. I spend most of my time in there."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man phoning a cinema.",q:"How many tickets does he buy?",o:["two","three","four"],a:1,x:"‘There were going to be four of us, but my sister can't come now, so just three.’",script:[["M","Hello, I'd like tickets for the half past seven film tonight, please. There were going to be four of us, but my sister can't come now, so just three. Two adults and one child."],["F","Three tickets – that's twenty-four pounds, please."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a girl talking about her weekend.",q:"What did she do on Sunday?",o:["She went to a museum.","She played tennis.","She visited her grandmother."],a:0,x:"Her friend had free tickets for the science museum, ‘so we went there instead’. Tennis and Grandma were on Saturday.",script:[["F","On Saturday I played tennis with my cousin in the morning, and then in the afternoon we visited Grandma. I was going to stay at home on Sunday, but my friend had free tickets for the science museum, so we went there instead. It was great."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a teacher talking to her students.",q:"What time will the bus leave?",o:["at 8.00","at 8.15","at 8.30"],a:1,x:"‘The bus will leave at a quarter past.’ Students must be at school by eight.",script:[["F","Right, everyone, tomorrow's trip. Please be at school by eight o'clock. We'll check that everyone's here, and the bus will leave at a quarter past. We should arrive at about half past nine."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man talking about his lost bag.",q:"Where did he leave his bag?",o:["on the train","in a café","at the gym"],a:1,x:"‘I went back to the café, and it was still there.’ He only thought he'd left it on the train.",script:[["M","I thought I'd left it on the train, so I called the station, but nobody had handed it in. Then I remembered I'd stopped for a coffee after the gym. I went back to the café, and it was still there, under the table!"]]},{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about what to eat.",q:"What will they eat?",o:["pizza","curry","salad"],a:2,x:"‘Let's just make a big salad, then.’ – ‘Good idea.’",script:[["M","Shall we get a pizza?"],["F","We had pizza yesterday. How about a curry?"],["M","It's so hot today, though. I don't really want anything heavy."],["F","True. Let's just make a big salad, then. There are lots of vegetables in the fridge."],["M","Good idea."]]}]},2:{extracts:[{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a concert.",q:"What did the boy think of the concert?",o:["The band played too loudly.","It was shorter than he expected.","The tickets were good value."],a:2,x:"‘For twenty pounds, I thought it was really worth the money.’ It lasted three hours, and loud is ‘normal’.",script:[["F","How was the concert?"],["M","Brilliant! It went on for nearly three hours, and they played all my favourite songs. It was loud, of course, but that's normal. And for twenty pounds, I thought it was really worth the money."],["F","Lucky you!"]]},{ctx:"You will hear a girl telling a friend about a new teacher.",q:"How does she feel about the teacher?",o:["She finds her lessons fun.","She thinks she's too strict.","She's disappointed with her."],a:0,x:"‘Her lessons are actually fun – she uses games and puzzles.’",script:[["M","What's the new maths teacher like?"],["F","I was worried, because everyone said she was really strict. And she does give lots of homework. But her lessons are actually fun – she uses games and puzzles, and the time goes really quickly."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man talking to a friend about his holiday.",q:"What was the problem with the holiday?",o:["the weather","the hotel","the food"],a:1,x:"‘The hotel was awful – the room was dirty.’ The weather was perfect and the restaurants were great.",script:[["F","Did you have a good holiday?"],["M","The beach was beautiful, and the weather was perfect every day. The restaurants were great too. But the hotel was awful – the room was dirty and the air conditioning didn't work. We complained, but they didn't do anything."]]},{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a TV programme.",q:"What do they agree about?",o:["It's too long.","The presenter is funny.","It's better than last year."],a:1,x:"He says ‘the presenter is really funny’ and she agrees: ‘She makes me laugh every week.’ They disagree about last year.",script:[["M","Did you watch the cooking programme last night?"],["F","Yes! I think it's better than last year's series."],["M","Hmm, I'm not sure. It's a bit long, too. But the presenter is really funny."],["F","Oh, she's brilliant. She makes me laugh every week."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a woman telling a friend about learning to swim.",q:"Why did she decide to learn?",o:["Her doctor told her to.","She's going on a beach holiday.","Her children wanted her to."],a:2,x:"‘My kids… kept asking me to come in the water with them.’ Her doctor's advice ‘isn't why’.",script:[["M","I didn't know you couldn't swim."],["F","I never learnt as a child. My doctor's always telling me I should do more exercise, but that's not why. My kids go swimming every week, and they kept asking me to come in the water with them. So I thought, why not?"]]},{ctx:"You will hear a boy talking to his sister about a lost key.",q:"What is he going to do?",o:["look for the key again","phone their dad again","wait at a neighbour's house"],a:2,x:"‘Mrs Brown next door said I can stay at her house… I'll go there now.’",script:[["F","Did you find your key?"],["M","No, I've looked everywhere. I tried calling Dad, but he's in a meeting until six."],["F","So what are you going to do?"],["M","Mrs Brown next door said I can stay at her house until someone gets home. I'll go there now."]]}]},3:{title:"Riverside Summer Festival",ctx:"You will hear a man talking on the radio about a summer festival in his town.",script:[["M",`Good morning! I'm Paul Grant from the Riverside Festival team, and I'm here to tell you about this year's festival, which takes place on the last weekend in July. It's our twentieth festival, so we're planning something special. As always, it's held in Victoria Park, next to the river.

The festival starts on Saturday at ten o'clock in the morning and finishes on Sunday evening with fireworks at half past nine. This year there'll be more than thirty bands playing on three stages, and for the first time we've got a special tent for comedy. There's plenty for children too – face painting, a magic show and a boat race on the river on Sunday afternoon.

Entry to the festival is free, but you'll need to buy a ticket if you want to use the car park, which costs five pounds a day. We'd really prefer people to come by bike or on foot if they can.

We're still looking for volunteers to help with things like picking up litter and giving out information. If you'd like to help, please email the festival office. Volunteers get a free T-shirt and lunch on both days!`]],items:[{s:"The festival takes place on the last weekend in {g}.",a:["July"],x:"‘the last weekend in July’"},{s:"This year is the festival's {g} year.",a:["twentieth","20th"],x:"‘It's our twentieth festival.’"},{s:"The fireworks on Sunday start at {g}.",a:["9.30","9:30","half past nine","nine thirty","21.30","21:30","9.30 pm","9:30 pm"],x:"‘fireworks at half past nine’ – ten o'clock is when it starts on Saturday."},{s:"New this year: a special tent for {g}.",a:["comedy"],x:"‘for the first time we've got a special tent for comedy’"},{s:"The car park costs {g} a day.",a:["£5","5","five pounds","5 pounds","£5.00"],x:"‘which costs five pounds a day’ – entry is free."},{s:"Volunteers get a free T-shirt and {g}.",a:["lunch","free lunch","a free lunch"],x:"‘Volunteers get a free T-shirt and lunch on both days.’"}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a teenager called Sophie Lane, who has won a prize for a short film.",script:[["M","Today I'm talking to sixteen-year-old Sophie Lane, whose short film has just won a national prize. Sophie, congratulations!"],["F","Thanks!"],["M","How did you get into film-making?"],["F","My uncle works in television, and when I was twelve he gave me an old video camera. At first I just filmed my cat and my little brother. Then I saw a competition for young film-makers online and decided to make a proper film."],["M","What's your prize-winning film about?"],["F","It's about an old man who teaches a teenager to play chess. I got the idea from my grandad, who taught me to play. I wanted to show that young and old people can learn a lot from each other."],["M","Was it difficult to make?"],["F","The filming was fine – we did it in four weekends. The hardest part was the editing. I'd never done it before, and I had to watch lots of online videos to learn how. I spent hours in my bedroom cutting and changing things."],["M","Who were the actors?"],["F","My grandad played the old man! He was really nervous at first, but he loved it in the end. And my friend Leo played the teenager. Neither of them had acted before."],["M","What did you win?"],["F","A week at a film studio in London, where I'll work with professional directors. I'm really excited. And my film will be shown at the cinema in my town, which is amazing."],["M","What's next for you?"],["F","I'd love to study film at university, but my parents want me to do something more practical as well. We've agreed I'll study film and business. That way I'll learn how to find money for my films!"]],qs:[{q:"How did Sophie start making films?",o:["She did a course at school.","Her uncle gave her a camera.","She won a competition."],a:1,x:"‘My uncle… gave me an old video camera.’ The competition came later."},{q:"Where did the idea for her film come from?",o:["a book she had read","her own grandfather","a news story"],a:1,x:"‘I got the idea from my grandad, who taught me to play.’"},{q:"What did Sophie find most difficult?",o:["finding actors","editing the film","filming at weekends"],a:1,x:"‘The hardest part was the editing.’ The filming ‘was fine’."},{q:"How did her grandfather feel about acting?",o:["He didn't want to do it.","He enjoyed it after a nervous start.","He found it boring."],a:1,x:"‘He was really nervous at first, but he loved it in the end.’"},{q:"What was Sophie's prize?",o:["some new film equipment","a week at a film studio","money for her next film"],a:1,x:"‘A week at a film studio in London.’"},{q:"What does Sophie plan to study?",o:["film only","film and business","acting"],a:1,x:"‘We've agreed I'll study film and business.’"}]}}}),LZ.data.lpapers.b1.push({id:"b1l4",title:"Listening Test 4",topics:"Everyday situations · A cookery course · Opinions · A volunteer in Peru",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You will hear a woman leaving a message for a friend.",q:"Where will they meet?",o:["at the bus stop","outside the station","in the car park"],a:1,x:"‘Let's meet outside the station instead.’ The car park is closed and the bus stop is too busy.",script:[["F","Hi, Kate, it's Lucy. About tomorrow – I know we said the car park, but it's closed for repairs all week. I thought about the bus stop, but it's always so crowded in the morning. Let's meet outside the station instead. See you at nine!"]]},{ctx:"You will hear a boy talking about his favourite sport.",q:"Which sport does he enjoy most?",o:["football","basketball","swimming"],a:1,x:"‘Basketball's the one I really love.’ He plays football because his friends do and swims for his health.",script:[["M","I play football on Saturdays because all my friends are in the team, and I go swimming twice a week because my doctor says it's good for my back. But basketball's the one I really love. I'd play it every day if I could."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man talking to a shop assistant.",q:"What is wrong with the jacket?",o:["It's the wrong colour.","It's too small.","A button is missing."],a:1,x:"‘It's a bit tight across the shoulders… Have you got a larger size?’ He likes the colour and the buttons are fine.",script:[["M","I bought this jacket last week. I love the colour, and I checked all the buttons before I left the shop. But when I got home I realised it's a bit tight across the shoulders. Have you got it in a larger size?"],["F","Let me have a look for you."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a girl talking to her father.",q:"How will she get to her friend's house?",o:["by car","on foot","by bike"],a:2,x:"‘I'll just take my bike.’ Her dad can't drive her, and it's too far to walk.",script:[["F","Dad, can you drive me to Emma's?"],["M","Sorry, love, the car's at the garage today."],["F","Oh. It's too far to walk, really. Well, it's not raining, so I'll just take my bike."],["M","Good idea. Don't forget your helmet."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a woman talking about a hotel.",q:"What did she like about the hotel?",o:["the food","the swimming pool","the staff"],a:2,x:"‘What made the stay special was the people who worked there.’ The food was ordinary and the pool was closed.",script:[["F","The food was nothing special, and the pool was closed while we were there, which was a shame. But what made the stay special was the people who worked there. They were so friendly and helpful – nothing was too much trouble."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man phoning a dentist.",q:"When is his appointment?",o:["Tuesday","Wednesday","Thursday"],a:2,x:"‘Thursday at two is fine.’ Tuesday was full and he works on Wednesday.",script:[["F","We haven't got anything on Tuesday, I'm afraid. How about Wednesday morning?"],["M","I work on Wednesdays. Is there anything later in the week?"],["F","Thursday at two o'clock?"],["M","Thursday at two is fine. Thanks."]]},{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a present.",q:"What will they buy for Sam?",o:["a video game","a T-shirt","a book"],a:2,x:"‘Let's get him that book about football, then.’ He has too many T-shirts, and games are too expensive.",script:[["F","What shall we get Sam? A T-shirt?"],["M","He's got loads of T-shirts. What about a video game?"],["F","They're really expensive, though."],["M","True. He's crazy about football – let's get him that book about football, then."],["F","Perfect."]]}]},2:{extracts:[{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a new café.",q:"What does the girl think of the café?",o:["It's too expensive.","The cakes are delicious.","It's too crowded."],a:1,x:"‘The cakes are amazing – the best I've ever had.’ She says the prices are ‘OK’.",script:[["M","Have you tried the new café on the corner?"],["F","Yes, I went on Saturday. It was quite busy, but we found a table. The prices are OK. And the cakes are amazing – the best I've ever had."],["M","I'll have to go, then!"]]},{ctx:"You will hear a boy telling a friend about a school trip.",q:"How did he feel during the trip?",o:["bored","tired","excited"],a:1,x:"‘I was so tired… I'd only slept about four hours.’ The trip itself was interesting.",script:[["F","How was the trip to the castle?"],["M","It was actually really interesting, but I was so tired I could hardly keep my eyes open. I'd only slept about four hours because I was worried about missing the bus."]]},{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a film.",q:"What does the man say about the film?",o:["The ending was surprising.","The actors were disappointing.","It was too long."],a:0,x:"‘I didn't see that ending coming at all.’ He says the actors were great and the time went quickly.",script:[["F","So, what did you think of the film?"],["M","Brilliant! The actors were great, and the two hours went really quickly. And I didn't see that ending coming at all – I was sure the brother was the thief!"]]},{ctx:"You will hear a woman talking to a friend about her new job.",q:"What does she like most about her new job?",o:["the salary","the people","the hours"],a:2,x:"‘The best thing is that I finish at three, so I can pick up the kids.’ The salary is the same, and she doesn't know her colleagues yet.",script:[["M","How's the new job going?"],["F","Really well. The money's about the same as before, and I haven't really got to know the people yet. But the best thing is that I finish at three, so I can pick up the kids from school."]]},{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about the weekend.",q:"What do they decide to do?",o:["go to the beach","go to the cinema","go for a walk"],a:2,x:"‘Let's walk up the hill and have a picnic.’ – ‘OK.’ The beach will be too crowded and the cinema is ‘a waste of a nice day’.",script:[["M","It's going to be sunny on Saturday. Shall we go to the beach?"],["F","Everyone else will be there too. It'll be packed."],["M","The cinema, then?"],["F","It seems a waste of a nice day to sit inside. Let's walk up the hill and have a picnic."],["M","OK, that sounds good."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a girl telling her mother about an exam.",q:"How does she feel about the exam?",o:["relieved","disappointed","nervous"],a:0,x:"‘I'm just glad it's finished – I was really worried about it.’",script:[["F","Mum, the exam's over!"],["M","How did it go?"],["F","Not bad, I think. The last question was hard, but I answered everything. I'm just glad it's finished – I was really worried about it all week."]]}]},3:{title:"Cookery course for teenagers",ctx:"You will hear a woman talking about a cookery course for teenagers.",script:[["F",`Hello, everyone. My name's Anna Webb, and I run the Young Cooks course here at the community centre. It's for teenagers aged between thirteen and seventeen, and it starts on the fifth of October.

The course lasts eight weeks, and classes are on Wednesday evenings from six to eight. You don't need any experience – we start with simple things like soups and salads, and by the end you'll be able to cook a three-course meal for your family.

In the last week, there's a competition, and the winner gets a cookery book signed by a famous chef, Tom Harris, who used to be a student here himself.

The course costs forty pounds, and that includes all the ingredients. You just need to bring an apron and a container to take your food home in.

We only have twelve places, so book soon! You can book online or at the reception desk, which is open every day except Sunday.`]],items:[{s:"The course starts on {g} October.",a:["5th","5","fifth","the fifth","5th of"],x:"‘it starts on the fifth of October’"},{s:"Classes are on {g} evenings.",a:["Wednesday","Wednesdays"],x:"‘classes are on Wednesday evenings from six to eight’"},{s:"By the end, students can cook a three-course {g}.",a:["meal","dinner"],x:"‘you'll be able to cook a three-course meal for your family’"},{s:"The competition prize is a signed {g}.",a:["cookery book","cookbook","book","recipe book"],x:"‘the winner gets a cookery book signed by a famous chef’"},{s:"The course costs £{g}, including ingredients.",a:["40","forty","£40"],x:"‘The course costs forty pounds.’"},{s:"Students should bring an apron and a {g}.",a:["container","food container","box"],x:"‘bring an apron and a container to take your food home in’"}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a young man called Jack Dale, who spent six months as a volunteer in Peru.",script:[["F","My guest today is Jack Dale, who's just come back from six months in Peru. Jack, why did you decide to go?"],["M","I'd finished school and I wasn't sure what to study at university. My cousin had volunteered in South America the year before, and she said it changed her life. So I thought it might help me decide."],["F","What did you do there?"],["M","I worked in a small village school in the mountains, helping the teachers with English lessons. I also helped build a new classroom – I'd never done anything like that before!"],["F","What was the most difficult thing?"],["M","Everyone expects me to say the food or the altitude, but I got used to those quite quickly. It was the language. I'd done a bit of Spanish at school, but the people in the village spoke very fast, and some spoke Quechua, a local language. For the first month I understood almost nothing."],["F","Where did you live?"],["M","With a family – a mother, a father and four children. They didn't have much money, but they were so generous. They treated me like a son. We still talk on video every Sunday."],["F","What's the best memory you have?"],["M","On my last day, the children at the school sang a song they'd written for me. I'm not usually emotional, but I couldn't stop crying!"],["F","And did the trip help you decide what to study?"],["M","Yes, definitely. I realised how much I enjoyed working with children, so I'm going to train to be a primary school teacher. I start in September."]],qs:[{q:"Why did Jack decide to go to Peru?",o:["He wanted to improve his Spanish.","He hoped it would help him choose a course.","His school organised the trip."],a:1,x:"‘I wasn't sure what to study… I thought it might help me decide.’"},{q:"What did Jack do in the village?",o:["He helped with English classes.","He taught sport.","He worked on a farm."],a:0,x:"‘helping the teachers with English lessons’"},{q:"What did Jack find most difficult?",o:["the food","the altitude","understanding people"],a:2,x:"‘It was the language… For the first month I understood almost nothing.’"},{q:"What does Jack say about the family he lived with?",o:["They wanted to learn English.","Their house was very small.","They were very kind to him."],a:2,x:"‘they were so generous. They treated me like a son.’"},{q:"What happened on Jack's last day?",o:["He gave a speech.","The children sang for him.","There was a party in the village."],a:1,x:"‘the children at the school sang a song they'd written for me’"},{q:"What is Jack going to do in September?",o:["go back to Peru","start teacher training","study Spanish"],a:1,x:"‘I'm going to train to be a primary school teacher. I start in September.’"}]}}}),LZ.data.lpapers.c1.push({id:"c1l1",title:"Listening Test 1",topics:"Three conversations · A sound designer · The value of boredom · Changing career",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear two friends discussing a documentary series about the ocean.",script:[["M","So, did you watch the rest of that ocean series in the end?"],["F","I did, and I was pleasantly surprised. I'd assumed it'd be the usual doom and gloom – dying reefs, sad music – and there was a bit of that, of course. But what really stood out was that they let the fishermen and the divers on those islands tell the story themselves. You don't often hear from the people whose livelihoods are actually on the line."],["M","Hmm, I'll grant you that. The science was pretty lightweight, though, wasn't it? Not that I minded."],["F","Well, it's hardly meant for marine biologists."],["M","Fair enough. My issue was more the ending. After fifty minutes of telling us how serious things are, it just… stopped. Not a word about what any of us could actually do about it. You're left feeling helpless, which surely isn't the point."],["F","I suppose they didn't want to lecture people."]],qs:[{q:"What does the woman feel the documentary did particularly well?",o:["It explained complex science in an accessible way.","It avoided exaggerating the threats facing the ocean.","It gave a voice to people who depend on the sea."],a:2,x:"‘What really stood out was that they let the fishermen and the divers… tell the story themselves.’ There was ‘a bit of’ doom and gloom, and the man calls the science ‘lightweight’."},{q:"The man thinks the documentary's main weakness was",o:["its reliance on dramatic images.","its lack of practical suggestions.","its narrow focus on one region."],a:1,x:"‘Not a word about what any of us could actually do about it.’ He ‘didn't mind’ the lightweight science."}]},{ctx:"You hear two colleagues talking about working in a shared office space.",script:[["F","I didn't realise you'd given up working from home altogether."],["M","Three months ago now. People assume it was the kids, but to be honest they're at school most of the day. It was more that I'd stopped being able to switch off – the laptop was on the kitchen table, so work just bled into everything. Now when I leave the building, that's it, I'm done. I did half hope I'd pick up some work from the people there, but that hasn't really happened."],["F","I've been going to the one near the station on Fridays. I'll be honest, I get far less done than I'd imagined – there's always someone wanting to chat, or a phone going off."],["M","Oh, tell me about it. I thought I'd be super-productive, and some days I barely write a line."],["F","Still, the coffee's decent."],["M","For what we pay, it ought to be!"]],qs:[{q:"Why did the man decide to rent a desk in a shared office?",o:["He wanted to separate his work from his home life.","He hoped to find new clients there.","His home had become too noisy to work in."],a:0,x:"‘I'd stopped being able to switch off… Now when I leave the building, that's it.’ Finding work was only a half-hope, and the children are at school."},{q:"What do they both think about the shared office?",o:["The facilities are not worth the cost.","It is harder to concentrate there than they expected.","The social side is its greatest benefit."],a:1,x:"She gets ‘far less done than I'd imagined’; he thought he'd be ‘super-productive’ but ‘some days I barely write a line’."}]},{ctx:"You hear two people talking about a novel they have both read.",script:[["M","You finished The Glass Harbour, then?"],["F","Last night. I have to say, I didn't warm to Nora at all to begin with – she's so cold with her sister. But by the last few chapters, when you understand what she gave up for the family, I found myself genuinely admiring her. I didn't see that coming."],["M","Same here. What did you make of the way it jumps around in time?"],["F","It drove me mad at first."],["M","I know people find it confusing, but I thought it was clever. Nora herself can't keep her memories in order – she's grieving, she's exhausted – so the book's all over the place because she is. It wouldn't work told in a straight line."],["F","I suppose that's true."]],qs:[{q:"What does the woman say about the novel's main character?",o:["She found her motives hard to believe.","She came to admire her as the novel went on.","She identified with her more than she had expected."],a:1,x:"‘I didn't warm to Nora at all to begin with… by the last few chapters… I found myself genuinely admiring her.’"},{q:"What is the man's opinion of the novel's structure?",o:["It is unnecessarily complicated.","It reflects the character's state of mind.","It makes the ending too predictable."],a:1,x:"‘Nora herself can't keep her memories in order… so the book's all over the place because she is.’"}]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a sound designer called Maya Brennan talking about her work in the film industry.",script:[["F",`Good evening. When I tell people I'm a sound designer, most of them assume I compose film music. I don't – that's a completely separate job. What I do is create everything else you hear: footsteps, doors, wind, the particular hum of a spaceship engine. And I got into it almost by accident. I'd trained as a radio journalist, and on my first placement I was sent to record a thunderstorm for a documentary. I spent the whole night on a hillside, and when I heard the playback, I realised I cared far more about the sound than the story.

People are often surprised to learn how little of what they hear in a film was recorded on set. On a typical drama, perhaps a third of the dialogue gets re-recorded later in a studio, and almost all the smaller sounds are added afterwards by people called Foley artists. They perform the sounds live while watching the film – walking on trays of gravel, rustling fabric, that kind of thing. One of my favourite tricks is that the sound of someone walking through snow is often made by squeezing a bag of cornflour. It sounds more like snow than real snow does.

The part of the job I find most demanding isn't the technical side, which you can learn. It's the patience. On my last film, I spent eleven days on a single scene – a two-minute sequence in a crowded market – layering nearly four hundred separate sounds so that it felt alive without drowning the conversation.

Silence, by the way, is one of the most powerful tools we have. But true silence on a cinema soundtrack actually makes audiences uncomfortable – they start to think the equipment has broken. So what we call silence is usually a very quiet background sound, known as room tone, which tells your brain you're still inside the world of the film.

Technology has changed things enormously. When I started, we cut magnetic tape by hand. Now I can build an entire forest on a laptop. The danger is that because everything is possible, sound can become over-designed – every leaf rustling, every breath exaggerated. The best advice I ever got came from an old editor who told me that sound should be felt rather than noticed.

If you're thinking of a career in this field, my advice is simple: start a library of your own recordings. Directors hire people whose sounds they can't find anywhere else. I still carry a small recorder everywhere I go – my family are used to me stopping in the street to record a squeaky gate.`]],items:[{s:"Maya originally trained to work as a {g}.",a:["radio journalist","journalist"],x:"‘I'd trained as a radio journalist’ – composing film music is a different job."},{s:"Maya discovered her interest in sound while recording a {g} at night.",a:["thunderstorm","storm"],x:"‘I was sent to record a thunderstorm for a documentary. I spent the whole night on a hillside.’"},{s:"In a typical drama, perhaps {g} of the dialogue is re-recorded in a studio.",a:["a third","one third","1/3","a third of it"],x:"‘perhaps a third of the dialogue gets re-recorded later in a studio’"},{s:"The sound of footsteps in snow is often made with a bag of {g}.",a:["cornflour","corn flour","cornstarch"],x:"‘walking through snow is often made by squeezing a bag of cornflour’ – gravel is used for other footsteps."},{s:"Maya says the most demanding aspect of her work is the {g} it requires.",a:["patience"],x:"‘It isn't the technical side… It's the patience.’"},{s:"Maya once spent eleven days on a scene set in a {g}.",a:["crowded market","market"],x:"‘a two-minute sequence in a crowded market’"},{s:"What sound designers call silence is usually a quiet sound known as {g}.",a:["room tone"],x:"‘a very quiet background sound, known as room tone’"},{s:"Maya advises newcomers to build a {g} of their own recordings.",a:["library","sound library"],x:"‘start a library of your own recordings’"}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear an interview in which a psychologist called Dr Adam Hale talks about boredom.",script:[["F","My guest today is the psychologist Dr Adam Hale, whose new book argues that boredom is not the enemy we think it is. Adam, why write a whole book about something so, well, dull?"],["M","I get that a lot. The honest answer is that I didn't set out to. I was researching attention – why we find it harder to concentrate than we used to – and I kept finding that the people who struggled most weren't the ones with the most distractions. They were the ones who couldn't tolerate even a few minutes of having nothing to do. Boredom kept turning up in the data, so eventually I stopped treating it as background noise and made it the subject."],["F","So what actually is boredom, from a psychologist's point of view?"],["M","The best definition I've found is that it's the uncomfortable feeling of wanting to be engaged in something satisfying and not being able to. It's not the same as having nothing to do – plenty of people sit on a beach doing nothing and feel perfectly content. Boredom is a kind of signal. It's your mind telling you that what you're doing isn't meeting your needs, and prompting you to change something."],["F","Then why do we try so hard to avoid it?"],["M","Because the signal is unpleasant, and now we have an instant way to switch it off. The phone in your pocket means you need never sit with the feeling for more than a second. The trouble is, that's a bit like taking painkillers for a broken leg without ever setting the bone. You feel better briefly, but nothing changes, and the restlessness comes back, often worse. In one of our studies, people who reached for their phones most often during a dull task reported feeling more bored at the end, not less."],["F","Your book has a chapter called ‘The Useful Void’. What's useful about it?"],["M","There's quite good evidence now that a period of boredom can boost creative thinking. In a well-known experiment, people who'd first done something mind-numbingly repetitive – copying numbers out of a phone directory – came up with more original uses for everyday objects afterwards than people who went straight to the task. I'd be cautious, though. The effect is real but modest, and it seems to depend on the mind being allowed to wander. If you're bored and anxious at the same time, you don't get any creative benefit at all."],["F","What about children? Parents often feel guilty if their kids are bored."],["M","I'd tell them to relax. Children who are constantly entertained by adults don't get the chance to discover what they actually enjoy. When my own daughter complains she's bored, I've learned not to rush in with suggestions. Nine times out of ten, twenty minutes later she's building something elaborate out of cardboard. The skill of occupying yourself is learned, and it can only be learned by being given the opportunity."],["F","Finally, has writing the book changed your own habits?"],["M","It has, a little. I used to fill every spare moment – podcasts on the walk to work, emails in the queue at the supermarket. Now I leave my phone in my bag on the train. The first week was genuinely hard; I hadn't realised how restless I'd become. But I've noticed I sleep better, and some of my better ideas for the book came while I was staring out of a train window. I won't pretend I've given up podcasts entirely, though."]],qs:[{q:"What led Adam to write about boredom?",o:["He wanted to challenge a popular theory about concentration.","It emerged unexpectedly from research on a related topic.","He had personal experience of long periods of boredom.","His publisher asked him to write about it."],a:1,x:"‘I didn't set out to. I was researching attention… Boredom kept turning up in the data.’"},{q:"How does Adam define boredom?",o:["as a state of complete mental inactivity","as a sign that someone lacks imagination","as a message that what we are doing is unfulfilling","as a reaction to being forced to wait"],a:2,x:"‘It's your mind telling you that what you're doing isn't meeting your needs.’ It's ‘not the same as having nothing to do’."},{q:"What point does Adam make with the comparison to painkillers?",o:["Phones deal with the symptom but not the underlying cause.","Some people become physically dependent on their phones.","Boredom can cause physical as well as mental discomfort.","Avoiding boredom is sometimes medically necessary."],a:0,x:"‘You feel better briefly, but nothing changes, and the restlessness comes back.’"},{q:"What is Adam's attitude to research linking boredom and creativity?",o:["He doubts whether the experiments were properly designed.","He believes its importance has been underestimated.","He accepts it but warns against overstating the effect.","He thinks it only applies to people in creative jobs."],a:2,x:"‘I'd be cautious, though. The effect is real but modest.’"},{q:"What does Adam suggest parents should do when children are bored?",o:["give them a range of activities to choose from","let them find their own way out of it","limit their use of screens during the day","explain to them why boredom is valuable"],a:1,x:"‘I've learned not to rush in with suggestions… The skill of occupying yourself… can only be learned by being given the opportunity.’"},{q:"How has Adam's behaviour changed since writing the book?",o:["He has stopped listening to podcasts altogether.","He now deliberately allows himself time without stimulation.","He has started taking the train instead of walking to work.","He finds it easier than before to concentrate on emails."],a:1,x:"‘Now I leave my phone in my bag on the train’ and stares out of the window. He hasn't ‘given up podcasts entirely’."}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people talk about changing career in their forties.",tasks:[{prompt:"For questions 21–25, choose from the list (A–H) what made each speaker decide to change career.",options:["a serious health problem","a conversation with a stranger","losing their previous job","a wish to spend more time outdoors","the example of a family member","frustration at a lack of recognition","a feeling of having achieved everything","an unexpected sum of money"]},{prompt:"For questions 26–30, choose from the list (A–H) what each speaker has found most challenging about their new career.",options:["being less experienced than colleagues","the physical demands of the work","earning far less money","other people's negative reactions","the length of the training","working irregular hours","having to learn new technology","a lack of job security"]}],speakers:[{a:[4,0],x:["E: ‘my aunt, at sixty-one, retrained as a midwife… what exactly is my excuse?’","A: ‘being on the ward with nurses half my age who knew infinitely more than I did’. The course itself ‘was fine’ (not E)."],script:[["F","I'd been an accountant for twenty years, and I was good at it, but I'd never have described it as a passion. Then my aunt, at sixty-one, retrained as a midwife, and I remember thinking – if she can start again at her age, what exactly is my excuse? So at forty-four I enrolled on a nursing course. The course itself was fine. What I found hardest, honestly, was being on the ward with nurses half my age who knew infinitely more than I did. I'd gone from being the person everyone asked to being the one asking all the questions."]]},{a:[1,3],x:["B: a furniture maker he sat next to on a delayed flight – ‘I went back to my desk… and couldn't stop thinking about it’.","D: ‘old colleagues who clearly thought I'd lost my mind… That wore me down more than any amount of sanding.’"],script:[["M","I was on a delayed flight, sitting next to a woman who turned out to be a furniture maker. We talked for three hours, and she described what it was like to make something with your hands that would outlast you. I went back to my desk at the bank on Monday and couldn't stop thinking about it. Within a year I'd handed in my notice. The workshop itself I love. What I wasn't prepared for was how people reacted – old colleagues who clearly thought I'd lost my mind, friends asking when I was going to get a proper job again. That wore me down more than any amount of sanding."]]},{a:[0,2],x:["A: ‘At forty-two I had a heart scare… The doctor was very blunt.’","C: ‘I earn about a quarter of what I used to.’"],script:[["F","At forty-two I had a heart scare – nothing fatal, thankfully, but it made me take a long look at the way I was living. Sixty-hour weeks in advertising, constant deadlines. The doctor was very blunt. So I retrained as a yoga teacher, which I'd done for years as a hobby. I'm healthier and far calmer, but I'd be lying if I said the money didn't bother me. I earn about a quarter of what I used to, and I've had to give up things I'd taken for granted, like holidays abroad."]]},{a:[5,5],x:["F: ‘every time a project went well, someone more senior took the credit… passed over for promotion for the third time’.","F: ‘the shifts – forecasting doesn't stop at night… plays havoc with my sleep’."],script:[["M","I'd spent fifteen years at a software company, and every time a project went well, someone more senior took the credit. After being passed over for promotion for the third time, I thought, enough. I'd always been fascinated by the weather, so I trained as a meteorologist. I'm valued now, which makes a huge difference. The one thing I still haven't adjusted to is the shifts. Forecasting doesn't stop at night, and working three nights in a row and then days again plays havoc with my sleep."]]},{a:[3,1],x:["D: ‘I'd barely seen daylight on a weekday since I was twenty-five. I wanted to work outside, simple as that.’","B: ‘nobody warned me how hard it is on the body… my back and knees let me know about it’."],script:[["F","I'd sat in the same grey office in the city for nineteen years, and I realised one spring that I'd barely seen daylight on a weekday since I was twenty-five. I wanted to work outside, simple as that. So I became a gardener – first on a big estate, now with my own small business. I adore it, but nobody warned me how hard it is on the body. At forty-seven, digging all day, my back and knees let me know about it every single evening."]]}]}}}),LZ.data.lpapers.c1.push({id:"c1l2",title:"Listening Test 2",topics:"Three conversations · Shipwreck archaeology · A zero-waste kitchen · A year abroad",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about an exhibition about robots.",script:[["M","Did you get to that robot exhibition in the end?"],["F","Saturday. I went expecting to spend ages on the bits where you can programme the robots yourself, but there were queues everywhere, so I gave up. What actually fascinated me was the first room – these clockwork figures from the eighteenth century that could write letters. I had no idea people were building that sort of thing so long ago."],["M","Oh, the writing boy! Yes, that was remarkable. I have to say, though, I came out feeling slightly manipulated."],["F","Manipulated?"],["M","Well, it was sponsored by a tech company, and it showed. Everything was about how robots will make our lives easier. Not one mention of the jobs they're already replacing. It felt more like an advert than an exhibition."],["F","I suppose I didn't think about who was paying for it."]],qs:[{q:"What did the woman find most interesting about the exhibition?",o:["the robots that visitors could control","the historical examples of early machines","the discussion of robots and employment"],a:1,x:"‘What actually fascinated me was the first room – these clockwork figures from the eighteenth century.’ She gave up on the interactive robots because of the queues."},{q:"The man criticises the exhibition for",o:["being aimed mainly at children.","being too crowded to enjoy.","presenting a one-sided view."],a:2,x:"‘Everything was about how robots will make our lives easier. Not one mention of the jobs they're already replacing.’"}]},{ctx:"You hear two neighbours talking about a plan to build new houses in their village.",script:[["F","Have you seen the notice about the forty new houses on the field behind the school?"],["M","I have. And look – my kids can't afford to live here; their friends have all moved to the city. Something has to be built somewhere. I'd rather it was done properly than not at all."],["F","I'm not against houses as such. But have you seen the drawings? Great big boxes with no gardens. They'll look completely out of place."],["M","They are a bit brutal."],["F","There's a consultation meeting next week. Rather than just objecting, I thought I'd take along some photos of the older cottages and propose they use similar materials and rooflines. They might listen if it's constructive."],["M","Good luck. Count me in if you need support."]],qs:[{q:"How does the man feel about the plan?",o:["resigned to it going ahead","angry that residents were not consulted","supportive of the need for new housing"],a:2,x:"‘My kids can't afford to live here… Something has to be built somewhere.’"},{q:"What does the woman intend to do?",o:["organise a petition against the plan","suggest changes to the design","attend a meeting just to find out more"],a:1,x:"‘Rather than just objecting… propose they use similar materials and rooflines.’ She is going to the meeting to make a proposal, not only to listen."}]},{ctx:"You hear a man and a woman talking about learning a musical instrument as an adult.",script:[["M","I hear you've taken up the piano."],["F","Eight months ago. Everyone assumes it's one of those keep-the-brain-young things, but no. My daughter plays the violin and she's got a concert next spring. Her teacher asked if anyone could play the piano part with her, and I thought, why not me? So I've got a deadline."],["M","That's lovely. I started the guitar at fifty. I tell you what, the practising isn't the problem – I love it. It's how painfully slowly things sink in. My son picked up in a month what's taken me a year."],["F","Oh, absolutely. I watch her learn a piece in a week and it takes me three. It's humbling."]],qs:[{q:"Why did the woman start learning the piano?",o:["to prove she could master a difficult skill","to be able to play alongside her daughter","to keep her mind active as she got older"],a:1,x:"Her daughter's teacher needed someone to play the piano part with her. She rejects the ‘keep-the-brain-young’ reason."},{q:"What do they agree about learning as an adult?",o:["Practising regularly is the hardest part.","Adults are more motivated than children.","Progress is slower than it is for children."],a:2,x:"He: ‘how painfully slowly things sink in’; she: ‘I watch her learn a piece in a week and it takes me three.’ He says the practising ‘isn't the problem’."}]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a marine archaeologist called Tom Reyes talking about his work on shipwrecks.",script:[["M",`Thanks for coming along tonight. I've been diving on shipwrecks for nearly twenty years, and I still get the same feeling every time I drop down through the water and a hull starts to appear out of the gloom. People imagine the job is all about treasure. In reality, the most valuable things we bring up are usually ordinary objects – a piece of rope, a sailor's shoe – things that tell us how people actually lived.

My route into the field was a little unusual. I studied history at university, but I'd never dived until I took a summer job as a dive instructor's assistant in Malta. By the end of that summer I'd seen my first wreck, and I knew exactly what I wanted to do.

The biggest challenge underwater isn't the depth or the cold. It's visibility. On some of the sites in the North Sea you can barely see your own hand, so we often work by touch, and we map the site using sound rather than sight.

Time is the other great enemy. At thirty metres, you might get just twenty minutes on the bottom before you have to begin the slow ascent. So every dive is planned to the minute, and each diver has one specific task.

Our most exciting recent project was a merchant ship that sank in 1702 off the Welsh coast. We'd expected to find cargo such as wine or cloth, but the hold was full of clay pipes – thousands of them, still packed in straw. They'd been made in Bristol and were on their way to Ireland.

Wood is our biggest headache once things have been raised. After three centuries underwater, timber is like a wet sponge – if you let it dry out naturally, it shrinks and cracks within hours. So everything goes straight into tanks of water, and conservation can take ten years or more.

Increasingly, we don't raise wrecks at all. We record them in extraordinary detail with 3D scanning and leave them where they are, which is often the best way to protect them. The scans also mean that people who will never dive can explore a wreck from their sofa.

And for anyone tempted to take up this work – learn to love paperwork. For every hour underwater, I spend about ten hours at a desk writing reports.`]],items:[{s:"Tom says the most valuable finds are usually {g} objects, such as rope.",a:["ordinary","everyday"],x:"‘the most valuable things we bring up are usually ordinary objects’ – not treasure."},{s:"Tom first dived while working as a {g}'s assistant in Malta.",a:["dive instructor","diving instructor"],x:"‘a summer job as a dive instructor's assistant in Malta’"},{s:"According to Tom, the biggest challenge underwater is {g}.",a:["visibility","poor visibility"],x:"‘The biggest challenge underwater isn't the depth or the cold. It's visibility.’"},{s:"At thirty metres, divers may have only {g} on the bottom.",a:["twenty minutes","20 minutes","20 mins","twenty mins"],x:"‘you might get just twenty minutes on the bottom’"},{s:"The ship that sank in 1702 was carrying thousands of {g}.",a:["clay pipes","pipes"],x:"‘the hold was full of clay pipes’ – wine and cloth were what they had expected."},{s:"The cargo was on its way from Bristol to {g}.",a:["Ireland"],x:"‘They'd been made in Bristol and were on their way to Ireland.’"},{s:"Tom compares wood that has been underwater for centuries to a {g}.",a:["wet sponge","sponge"],x:"‘timber is like a wet sponge’"},{s:"For every hour underwater, Tom spends about ten hours writing {g}.",a:["reports"],x:"‘I spend about ten hours at a desk writing reports.’"}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a chef called Nadia Osei, whose restaurant produces almost no waste.",script:[["M","My guest is Nadia Osei, whose London restaurant has become famous for producing almost no waste. Nadia, where did the idea come from?"],["F","Not from any grand principle, I'm afraid. When I opened the restaurant, money was desperately tight, and I was horrified by what we were throwing away every night – bags and bags of it. Every one of those bags was money I'd paid for. Reducing waste began as a way of surviving. The environmental argument came later, once I started reading about it."],["M","What did you change first?"],["F","The menu, which surprised people. Most chefs design a menu and then order whatever it needs. We turned that round. We see what the farmers have too much of that week – the misshapen carrots, a surplus of cabbages – and then we write the menu. It means the menu changes almost daily, which my staff found exhausting at first. Now they say it's the most creative part of the job."],["M","Some critics say zero-waste cooking is more about marketing than substance."],["F","I understand the scepticism. There are restaurants that put a few vegetable peelings on the plate and call themselves sustainable. But you can't fake it for long – the numbers don't lie. We weigh every bin, every day, and we publish the figures on our website. If people want to check, they can."],["M","What's the hardest thing to avoid wasting?"],["F","People assume it's food, but we've largely solved that – what we can't cook, we ferment or compost. It's packaging that defeats us. Some of our suppliers still deliver in plastic, and there's only so much you can persuade a small business to change when they're struggling themselves. I try not to be preachy about it."],["M","Do diners notice any of this?"],["F","Honestly, most don't, and I'm fine with that. They come because the food is good – or I hope so. If they left thinking about waste rather than about what they'd eaten, I'd feel I'd failed as a cook. The sustainability should be invisible, really. It's the kitchen's job, not the customer's."],["M","What's next for you?"],["F","I've been asked many times to open a second restaurant, and I've always said no. What I'm doing instead is running free courses for other chefs – twelve so far this year. One restaurant can only do so much. If a hundred kitchens each cut their waste by half, that's worth far more than anything I could achieve alone."]],qs:[{q:"What originally motivated Nadia to reduce waste?",o:["a documentary she had seen about food waste","the need to cut the restaurant's costs","pressure from her customers","her training at a previous restaurant"],a:1,x:"‘Every one of those bags was money I'd paid for. Reducing waste began as a way of surviving.’ The environmental argument ‘came later’."},{q:"How did Nadia's staff react to the new approach to the menu?",o:["They felt it limited their creativity.","They resisted it until customers responded well.","They were enthusiastic from the start.","They found it tiring at first but came to enjoy it."],a:3,x:"‘my staff found exhausting at first. Now they say it's the most creative part of the job.’"},{q:"How does Nadia respond to criticism of zero-waste restaurants?",o:["She admits that her restaurant has sometimes exaggerated its claims.","She accepts that some criticism is fair but says her results are open to checking.","She suggests that critics do not understand how kitchens work.","She believes marketing is a necessary part of the movement."],a:1,x:"‘I understand the scepticism… We weigh every bin, every day, and we publish the figures.’"},{q:"What does Nadia find most difficult to eliminate?",o:["food that customers leave on their plates","ingredients that cannot be preserved","packaging from suppliers","energy used in the kitchen"],a:2,x:"‘It's packaging that defeats us.’ Food waste is ‘largely solved’."},{q:"What is Nadia's view of her customers' awareness of her methods?",o:["She wishes more of them understood what she is trying to do.","She thinks they should be encouraged to reduce their own waste.","She feels it does not matter as long as they enjoy the food.","She worries that it spoils their experience."],a:2,x:"‘Most don't, and I'm fine with that… The sustainability should be invisible.’"},{q:"Why is Nadia running courses for other chefs?",o:["to earn extra income for her restaurant","because she has decided to stop cooking herself","to have a wider impact than one restaurant can","because other restaurants have copied her ideas badly"],a:2,x:"‘One restaurant can only do so much. If a hundred kitchens each cut their waste by half…’ The courses are free."}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people talk about spending a year living abroad.",tasks:[{prompt:"For questions 21–25, choose from the list (A–H) the main reason each speaker gives for moving abroad.",options:["to study a subject not available at home","to escape a stressful situation","to join a partner","to take up a job offer","to learn a language properly","to look after a relative","to fulfil a childhood ambition","to save money"]},{prompt:"For questions 26–30, choose from the list (A–H) what surprised each speaker most about living abroad.",options:["how quickly they felt at home","how much they missed familiar food","how different the working culture was","how hard it was to make local friends","how much the weather affected their mood","how expensive daily life was","how their view of their own country changed","how little they used the language they had learned"]}],speakers:[{a:[4,3],x:["E: ‘I'd done six years of Portuguese at school and could barely order a coffee, so… the only way to really learn it was to live in Lisbon.’","D: ‘getting invited into anyone's home… that took months. Most of my friends that year were other foreigners.’"],script:[["M","I'd done six years of Portuguese at school and could barely order a coffee, so I decided the only way to really learn it was to live in Lisbon for a year. I found a room, a part-time job in a bookshop, and I made myself speak it all day. What caught me off guard was the social side. People were perfectly friendly at work, but getting invited into anyone's home, getting beyond polite chat – that took months. Most of my friends that year were other foreigners, which wasn't the plan at all."]]},{a:[2,4],x:["C: ‘My husband got a post at a university in Helsinki, and I followed him.’","E: ‘what months of near-darkness does to you… my mood was on the floor’. The cold ‘was fine’."],script:[["F","My husband got a post at a university in Helsinki, and I followed him – I work freelance, so I could work from anywhere. I'd prepared myself for the cold, and actually the cold was fine. What nobody tells you is what months of near-darkness does to you. By December I was sleeping ten hours and still exhausted, and my mood was on the floor. Then in May the light came back and I felt like a different person."]]},{a:[3,2],x:["D: ‘I was headhunted by an engineering firm in Tokyo – the salary was too good to turn down.’","C: ‘What really threw me was the office… the unwritten rules.’ Food and language were ‘no problem’."],script:[["M","I was headhunted by an engineering firm in Tokyo – the salary was too good to turn down. I'd visited Japan as a tourist, so I thought I knew what to expect. Food, language, trains – no problem. What really threw me was the office. Meetings where the real decision had clearly been made beforehand, colleagues who wouldn't leave until the boss did. It took me a long time to understand the unwritten rules."]]},{a:[1,6],x:["B: ‘a divorce, a business that went under – I needed to get away from everything that reminded me of it.’","G: ‘I came back seeing it through their eyes… It's never looked quite the same since.’"],script:[["F","I'd just come out of a very difficult few years – a divorce, a business that went under – and I needed to get away from everything that reminded me of it. A friend had a flat in Buenos Aires she wasn't using. I went for a year and did very little except walk and read. The strange thing was coming home. I'd spent twelve months hearing people talk about my country, and I came back seeing it through their eyes – the good and the not-so-good. It's never looked quite the same since."]]},{a:[6,0],x:["G: ‘Ever since I was about eight… I'd wanted to live there.’","A: ‘within about three weeks I felt like I'd lived there all my life’. He had expected to be homesick."],script:[["M","Ever since I was about eight and saw a documentary about the Canadian Rockies, I'd wanted to live there. At forty I finally did it – a year's contract at a ski resort in Alberta. I'd expected to feel homesick, especially at Christmas. But honestly, within about three weeks I felt like I'd lived there all my life. I had a local coffee shop; people knew my name. I nearly didn't come back."]]}]}}}),LZ.data.lpapers.c1.push({id:"c1l3",title:"Listening Test 3",topics:"Three conversations · Urban beekeeping · A documentary photographer · Life without a car",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear two colleagues talking about working from home.",script:[["F","So, three months of working from home. How are you finding it?"],["M","Honestly? The work itself is going better than ever. No interruptions, no one wandering over for a chat just as I've got into something. My output has probably doubled."],["F","But?"],["M","But I hadn't realised how much of what I learnt came from overhearing things. Just sitting near the senior people, hearing how they handled a difficult client on the phone. You don't get that on a video call."],["F","I know what you mean. For me it's the opposite, though. I'm constantly distracted at home – the kids, the washing, the fridge. I've actually started coming in two days a week just to get something done."]],qs:[{q:"What does the man miss about working in the office?",o:["having a clear boundary between work and home","the social life with his team","the chance to learn informally from colleagues"],a:2,x:"‘how much of what I learnt came from overhearing things… hearing how they handled a difficult client’. He doesn't miss the chats – they were interruptions."},{q:"The woman has started going into the office because",o:["her manager asked her to.","she finds it easier to concentrate there.","she wants to see her colleagues."],a:1,x:"‘I'm constantly distracted at home… I've started coming in two days a week just to get something done.’"}]},{ctx:"You hear a man and a woman discussing a novel they have both read.",script:[["M","Did you finish it?"],["F","Last night. I have to say, I was ready to give up after the first fifty pages – all that description of the town, the weather, the history of the family. But once the brother came back, I couldn't put it down."],["M","Same here. Though I thought the ending let it down. After all that tension, everything was tied up so neatly. Everyone forgiven, everyone happy. Real families don't work like that."],["F","Hmm. I'd have agreed with you if it hadn't been for the last page. Did you notice that the mother never actually speaks to him again? I think the author's telling us it's not as resolved as it looks."],["M","Oh. I must have missed that."]],qs:[{q:"What did the woman initially dislike about the novel?",o:["its complicated plot","its unlikeable characters","its slow opening"],a:2,x:"‘I was ready to give up after the first fifty pages – all that description’."},{q:"What does the woman suggest about the ending?",o:["It was unrealistic, as the man says.","It was predictable from the start.","It was more ambiguous than the man thinks."],a:2,x:"‘the mother never actually speaks to him again… it's not as resolved as it looks.’"}]},{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about running a marathon.",script:[["F","I can't believe you're actually doing a marathon. You used to hate running!"],["M","I still don't love it, to be honest. But my dad had a heart operation last year, and the hospital was incredible. The race is raising money for their cardiac unit. That's what gets me out of bed at six."],["F","How's the training going?"],["M","My legs are coping fine – I'm quite surprised. It's my head that's the problem. Around the twenty-kilometre mark I start telling myself I can't do it, and it takes everything I've got to keep going. I've started listening to podcasts, which helps a bit."],["F","Well, I'll be at the finish line with a sign."]],qs:[{q:"Why is the man running the marathon?",o:["to raise funds for a hospital","to get fit after an illness","to prove something to his father"],a:0,x:"‘The race is raising money for their cardiac unit. That's what gets me out of bed.’ It was his father who was ill."},{q:"What does he find hardest about training?",o:["getting up early","staying mentally motivated","coping with injuries"],a:1,x:"‘My legs are coping fine… It's my head that's the problem.’"}]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a woman called Clara Finch talking about keeping bees on city rooftops.",script:[["F",`Good evening, everyone. I'm Clara Finch, and for the last eight years I've kept bees on the roofs of office buildings in the centre of Manchester. People are often amazed that bees can survive in a city at all. In fact, they tend to do better here than in the countryside, because city gardens and parks offer a far greater variety of flowers than fields planted with a single crop.

I got into beekeeping almost by accident. I was working as an accountant, and our building manager wanted to put hives on the roof as part of a green initiative. Nobody else volunteered, so I did. Within a year, I'd left my job.

The hardest part of rooftop beekeeping isn't the bees – it's the wind. At twenty storeys up, a strong gust can knock a hive over, so every one of ours is strapped down with heavy straps.

People always ask whether the honey tastes of pollution. It doesn't. Bees filter out almost everything, and our honey has been tested by a laboratory every year. What it does taste of is lime trees, which line many of the city's streets – in July it's quite distinctive.

One of our biggest successes has been with schools. We've taken more than three thousand children onto the rooftops, and for most of them it's the first time they've seen where honey actually comes from.

I should add a warning, though. Rooftop hives have become fashionable, and some cities now have more bees than the flowers can support. That's bad news for wild bees, which have to compete for the same food. So before setting up a hive, find out what else is in your area.

If you'd like to get involved, the best first step is to join a local beekeeping association. Most of them run a beginners' course in spring, and many will lend you equipment for your first season.`]],items:[{s:"City bees can find a greater {g} of flowers than those in the countryside.",a:["variety","range","diversity"],x:"‘city gardens and parks offer a far greater variety of flowers’"},{s:"Before becoming a beekeeper, Clara worked as an {g}.",a:["accountant"],x:"‘I was working as an accountant’"},{s:"The biggest problem for rooftop hives is the {g}.",a:["wind","strong wind","winds"],x:"‘The hardest part of rooftop beekeeping isn't the bees – it's the wind.’"},{s:"Every hive is secured with heavy {g}.",a:["straps"],x:"‘every one of ours is strapped down with heavy straps’"},{s:"In July, the honey tastes of {g}.",a:["lime trees","lime","limes","lime tree"],x:"‘What it does taste of is lime trees… in July it's quite distinctive.’"},{s:"More than {g} children have visited the rooftop hives.",a:["3,000","3000","three thousand"],x:"‘We've taken more than three thousand children onto the rooftops’"},{s:"Too many hives in a city can be harmful to {g}.",a:["wild bees","wild bee"],x:"‘That's bad news for wild bees, which have to compete for the same food.’"},{s:"Many associations will lend beginners {g} for their first season.",a:["equipment","kit","their equipment"],x:"‘many will lend you equipment for your first season’"}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a documentary photographer called Daniel Mercer.",script:[["F","My guest is Daniel Mercer, whose photographs of fishing communities have just been published as a book. Daniel, how did the project start?"],["M","I'd been commissioned by a magazine to take a single portrait of a trawlerman in Cornwall – a one-day job. But when I got there, he told me his was the last boat in the harbour, and that in ten years there'd be no fishing there at all. I realised I was looking at something that was disappearing, and I felt someone ought to record it properly. That one day turned into four years."],["F","You spent a lot of time at sea with the fishermen. Was that difficult?"],["M","Physically, yes – I was seasick for the first six trips. But the real challenge was earning their trust. They'd had journalists before who'd portrayed them as either heroes or dinosaurs. For the first few months I barely took any pictures at all. I just helped with the nets and kept my mouth shut."],["F","Your photographs are all in black and white. Why?"],["M","People assume it's to make them look timeless or romantic, and I'm actually rather wary of that. The real reason is practical. On deck, the light changes every few seconds, and the colours were a mess – orange oilskins, blue nets, green water. In black and white, you see the faces and the work. The colour was getting in the way."],["F","Some critics have said the book is too sad."],["M","I can see why. There's a lot of loss in it. But I'd say it's a book about pride more than sadness. These people do an extraordinarily hard job with enormous skill. If readers only come away feeling sorry for them, then I haven't done my job."],["F","Has the book changed anything?"],["M","It's too early to say whether it'll make any difference to fishing policy – I'm not naive about that. But something unexpected has happened. Young people from the town have been in touch saying they'd never really understood what their grandparents did, and several have asked to go out on the boats. That's worth more to me than any review."],["F","What's next?"],["M","I'm going to do the same thing with a mining community in Wales. But this time I'm going to hand out cameras to the miners' families as well. I want the people in the pictures to have a say in how they're shown."]],qs:[{q:"What made Daniel decide to extend his original assignment?",o:["The magazine asked him to produce a longer feature.","He realised a way of life was coming to an end.","The fisherman invited him to stay in the town.","He wanted to challenge negative images of fishing."],a:1,x:"‘his was the last boat in the harbour… I realised I was looking at something that was disappearing.’"},{q:"What does Daniel say was most difficult about working with the fishermen?",o:["coping with seasickness","learning to help with the fishing","gaining their confidence","taking photographs in bad weather"],a:2,x:"‘the real challenge was earning their trust’. Seasickness was only physical and short-lived."},{q:"Why did Daniel choose to work in black and white?",o:["to give the images a romantic quality","because colour distracted from what mattered","because it was cheaper to print","to make the book look like older photographs"],a:1,x:"‘In black and white, you see the faces and the work. The colour was getting in the way.’ He is wary of the romantic reading."},{q:"How does Daniel respond to the criticism that the book is too sad?",o:["He suggests critics have misunderstood the fishermen.","He admits he should have included more positive images.","He says sadness was unavoidable given the subject.","He argues that its main theme is something else."],a:3,x:"‘I'd say it's a book about pride more than sadness.’"},{q:"What has pleased Daniel most since the book came out?",o:["its influence on government policy","the positive reviews it has received","the interest shown by young local people","the fishermen's reaction to their portraits"],a:2,x:"‘Young people from the town… have asked to go out on the boats. That's worth more to me than any review.’"},{q:"What will Daniel do differently in his next project?",o:["work more quickly","involve the community in taking the photographs","use colour photography","focus on a single family"],a:1,x:"‘this time I'm going to hand out cameras to the miners' families as well.’"}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people talk about giving up their car.",tasks:[{prompt:"For questions 21–25, choose from the list (A–H) the main reason each speaker gives for getting rid of their car.",options:["the cost of running it","concern about pollution","moving to a city centre","being involved in an accident","wanting to get fitter","pressure from a partner","the car breaking down","starting a job close to home"]},{prompt:"For questions 26–30, choose from the list (A–H) what has surprised each speaker most since giving up their car.",options:["how much time they have saved","how much fitter they have become","how well they now know their local area","how complicated shopping has become","how much money they have saved","how often friends offer them lifts","how reliable public transport is","how rarely they miss having a car"]}],speakers:[{a:[0,2],x:["A: ‘the insurance went up for the third year running, and the parking permit… doubled… I simply couldn't justify it any more.’","C: ‘I know the names of half the shopkeepers on my street now… I'd been driving past all of it for years.’"],script:[["F","When the insurance went up for the third year running, and the parking permit for my street doubled on top of that, I simply couldn't justify it any more. The car was sitting outside my house six days a week. I assumed I'd spend my first months car-free counting the money I was saving, but honestly, I hardly think about that. What's struck me is that I now walk everywhere, and I know the names of half the shopkeepers on my street. There's a bakery two minutes away I'd been driving past for years without noticing."]]},{a:[3,6],x:["D: ‘after I went into the back of someone at a roundabout… I just didn't trust myself behind the wheel.’","G: ‘I'd always assumed the buses round here were hopeless… they've let me down once in a year.’"],script:[["M","Nobody was hurt, thank goodness, but after I went into the back of someone at a roundabout, something changed. I just didn't trust myself behind the wheel any more, and I sold the car a month later. My wife thought I'd buy another within weeks. I'd always assumed the buses round here were hopeless – everyone complains about them – but they've let me down once in a year. Once! I've actually started defending them at dinner parties, which I never expected."]]},{a:[1,1],x:["B: ‘I read a report about the air on the road outside my children's school… I couldn't keep adding to it.’","B: ‘I've lost six kilos without even trying… my doctor was amazed.’"],script:[["F","I read a report about the quality of the air on the road outside my children's school, and it genuinely frightened me. I was one of the parents sitting in a queue of cars with the engine running every morning, and I couldn't keep adding to it. So we got cargo bikes instead. I'd expected it to be exhausting, and at first it was. But I've lost six kilos without even trying, and at my last check-up my doctor was amazed at my blood pressure. That was a lovely surprise."]]},{a:[2,3],x:["C: ‘when we moved into a flat right in the centre… there was nowhere to park it anyway’.","D: ‘The weekly shop is the real headache… it's become a major operation.’"],script:[["M","It wasn't a principled decision, I'm afraid. When we moved into a flat right in the centre of town, there was nowhere to park anyway – the nearest space was a twenty-minute walk and cost a fortune. So it went. Getting to work is easier than ever. The weekly shop is the real headache, though. You can't carry a week's food home on foot, so it's either several trips or ordering online and waiting in for a delivery. It's become a major operation."]]},{a:[6,7],x:["G: ‘the engine finally died on the motorway… I decided not to replace it’.","H: ‘I thought I'd be desperate for one within a month… I honestly can't remember the last time I wished I had it.’"],script:[["M","My old car's engine finally died on the motorway one Friday evening, and while I was waiting for the recovery truck, I thought: do I really need another one? I decided not to replace it, just to see. I thought I'd be desperate for one within a month – I'd driven since I was seventeen. But I honestly can't remember the last time I wished I had it. I rent one for the odd weekend away, and that's it. Friends keep asking when I'm buying a new one, and the answer is: probably never."]]}]}}}),LZ.data.lpapers.c1.push({id:"c1l4",title:"Listening Test 4",topics:"Three conversations · Restoring old maps · A sleep scientist · Learning something new",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about a restaurant they have just visited.",script:[["M","Well? Was it worth the three-month wait for a table?"],["F","The food, yes, absolutely. That lamb dish was extraordinary. But I couldn't relax. The waiters hovered over us the whole time, explaining every ingredient in great detail. I just wanted to talk to you."],["M","I didn't mind that, actually – I found it quite interesting. What got to me was the bill. I don't object to paying for good food, but there was a fifteen per cent charge added on top that nobody mentioned when we booked."],["F","Really? I didn't even look."]],qs:[{q:"What spoilt the meal for the woman?",o:["the quality of the food","the attentiveness of the staff","the time she had to wait for a table"],a:1,x:"‘The waiters hovered over us the whole time… I just wanted to talk to you.’ The food was ‘extraordinary’."},{q:"What was the man unhappy about?",o:["the overall price of the meal","a charge that wasn't made clear in advance","the explanations given by the waiters"],a:1,x:"‘I don't object to paying for good food, but there was a fifteen per cent charge… that nobody mentioned.’"}]},{ctx:"You hear two students discussing a group project.",script:[["F","How's the project going with your group?"],["M","Don't ask. There are four of us, and two of them have done absolutely nothing. I've ended up writing most of the report myself."],["F","Have you told the tutor?"],["M","I thought about it, but I don't want to look like I'm complaining. And it's partly my own fault – I took over too quickly at the start because I was worried about the deadline. They probably thought I didn't want their help."],["F","Maybe. But you still need to sort it out before the presentation. You can't do that on your own."],["M","True. I'm going to ask them each to take one section of the talk. At least that way it's clear who's responsible."]],qs:[{q:"How does the man feel about the situation?",o:["He blames the tutor for creating the groups.","He accepts he has contributed to the problem.","He is determined to report the others."],a:1,x:"‘it's partly my own fault – I took over too quickly at the start’."},{q:"What does the man decide to do?",o:["ask the tutor for more time","prepare the presentation himself","give each member a specific task"],a:2,x:"‘I'm going to ask them each to take one section of the talk.’"}]},{ctx:"You hear a man and a woman talking about a TV documentary about climate change.",script:[["F","Did you see the documentary about the glaciers last night?"],["M","I did. The photography was stunning, but I'm not sure what they were trying to achieve. It was just an hour of bad news. By the end I felt so helpless I switched it off."],["F","I know people say that, but I think the problem is we're not scared enough. If a programme like that makes people uncomfortable, good."],["M","But people don't act when they're frightened – they switch off, like I did. If they'd shown some of the things that are actually working, people might feel they could do something."],["F","Hmm. Maybe there's room for both."]],qs:[{q:"What was the man's reaction to the documentary?",o:["He felt it exaggerated the problem.","He found it too depressing to continue watching.","He was impressed by its message."],a:1,x:"‘By the end I felt so helpless I switched it off.’"},{q:"The man thinks documentaries on this subject should",o:["focus on the scientific evidence.","include examples of successful solutions.","avoid showing distressing images."],a:1,x:"‘If they'd shown some of the things that are actually working, people might feel they could do something.’"}]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a man called Oliver Grant talking about his work restoring old maps.",script:[["M",`Hello, everyone. I'm Oliver Grant, and I restore historic maps – mostly for libraries and private collectors, occasionally for museums. The oldest map I've worked on was drawn in 1480, and it had spent the previous century folded up inside a book, which is why it survived at all.

I trained originally as a bookbinder, and that's still the most useful background for this work, because so many old maps were bound into atlases. Understanding how paper was folded and stitched tells you a great deal about a map's history.

The most common damage I see isn't from age at all. It's from earlier repairs. For much of the twentieth century, people fixed tears with sticky tape, and that tape turns yellow and brittle over time and leaves a stain that's almost impossible to remove.

To clean a map, I often use nothing more than a soft brush and a special kind of rubber. The golden rule of restoration is that anything I do must be reversible, so that a restorer in a hundred years' time can undo my work if they find a better method.

Maps are also fascinating for their mistakes. One seventeenth-century map I restored showed California as an island. That error was copied by mapmakers for nearly a hundred years, simply because nobody went to check.

My favourite part of the job, though, is finding things that aren't meant to be there. People wrote notes in the margins, and I once found a list of shopping on the back of a map of Scotland, dated 1790.

The work is slow. A single large map can take me six months. And the market has changed: collectors now want maps in their original condition, and they'll often pay more for a map with honest damage than for one that's been over-restored.

If you own an old map, please don't frame it in direct sunlight. Light fades the colours faster than anything else.`]],items:[{s:"The oldest map Oliver has restored survived because it was kept inside a {g}.",a:["book"],x:"‘it had spent the previous century folded up inside a book’"},{s:"Oliver's original training was as a {g}.",a:["bookbinder","book binder"],x:"‘I trained originally as a bookbinder’"},{s:"The most common damage is caused by earlier repairs using {g}.",a:["sticky tape","tape"],x:"‘people fixed tears with sticky tape’ – not by age."},{s:"Oliver's golden rule is that all restoration must be {g}.",a:["reversible"],x:"‘anything I do must be reversible’"},{s:"A seventeenth-century map wrongly showed {g} as an island.",a:["California"],x:"‘One seventeenth-century map I restored showed California as an island.’"},{s:"On the back of one map, Oliver found a {g} from 1790.",a:["shopping list","list of shopping"],x:"‘I once found a list of shopping on the back of a map of Scotland, dated 1790.’"},{s:"A single large map can take up to {g} to restore.",a:["six months","6 months"],x:"‘A single large map can take me six months.’"},{s:"Maps should not be framed in {g}.",a:["direct sunlight","sunlight"],x:"‘please don't frame it in direct sunlight’"}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a sleep scientist called Dr Helen Park.",script:[["M","My guest today is Dr Helen Park, who has spent twenty years studying sleep. Helen, what first drew you to the subject?"],["F","It was very personal, actually. I was a junior doctor working thirty-six-hour shifts, and I made a mistake with a patient's medication – thankfully caught in time. It shook me. I started reading about what lack of sleep does to decision-making, and I never really stopped."],["M","We hear a lot about needing eight hours. Is that right?"],["F","It's a useful average, but it's not a rule. Some people genuinely need seven, some need nine. What matters more than the exact number is regularity – going to bed and getting up at roughly the same time, even at weekends. That's the thing most people get wrong."],["M","What about phones in the bedroom?"],["F","Everyone blames the blue light from screens, and it does have some effect. But in our research, the bigger problem was what people were doing on their phones. Reading work emails or arguing on social media at eleven at night winds you up. A calm film on a tablet is far less harmful than one stressful message."],["M","Is it true that teenagers are naturally lazy about getting up?"],["F","No, and that's a myth I'd love to get rid of. During adolescence the body clock genuinely shifts later by two hours or so. Asking a sixteen-year-old to be alert at eight in the morning is like asking an adult to be alert at six. It's biology, not laziness. Some schools that have started later have seen real improvements in results."],["M","Do you follow your own advice?"],["F","Mostly. I'm very strict about my bedtime. The one thing I struggle with is caffeine – I love a coffee after lunch, and I know perfectly well it's still in my system at bedtime. I'm working on it."],["M","What would you like to see change?"],["F","I'd like employers to take sleep as seriously as they take safety. We'd never let someone operate machinery after four glasses of wine, but we routinely let exhausted people make decisions that affect others. Being awake for twenty hours has a similar effect on performance."]],qs:[{q:"What led Helen to study sleep?",o:["a course she took as a medical student","an incident when she was working as a doctor","her own long-term sleep problems","a patient who suffered from insomnia"],a:1,x:"‘I made a mistake with a patient's medication… It shook me.’"},{q:"According to Helen, what is the most common mistake people make?",o:["sleeping fewer than eight hours","sleeping too much at weekends","not keeping a regular sleep routine","going to bed too early"],a:2,x:"‘What matters more… is regularity… That's the thing most people get wrong.’"},{q:"What did Helen's research find about phones?",o:["Blue light is the main cause of poor sleep.","The content people engage with matters more than the screen.","Watching films before bed is as harmful as reading emails.","Phones should never be kept in the bedroom."],a:1,x:"‘the bigger problem was what people were doing on their phones’. Blue light has only ‘some effect’."},{q:"What point does Helen make about teenagers?",o:["They need more sleep than adults.","Their behaviour is caused by a natural change in their body.","Schools should reduce their homework.","They use technology more than adults do."],a:1,x:"‘During adolescence the body clock genuinely shifts later… It's biology, not laziness.’"},{q:"What does Helen admit about her own habits?",o:["She often goes to bed late.","She finds it hard to give up afternoon coffee.","She works too late in the evening.","She uses her phone in bed."],a:1,x:"‘The one thing I struggle with is caffeine – I love a coffee after lunch.’ She's ‘very strict about my bedtime’."},{q:"What change would Helen most like to see?",o:["more research into sleep disorders","shorter working hours for doctors","employers treating tiredness as a safety issue","later start times for all schools"],a:2,x:"‘I'd like employers to take sleep as seriously as they take safety.’"}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people talk about learning a new skill as an adult.",tasks:[{prompt:"For questions 21–25, choose from the list (A–H) what each speaker learnt.",options:["a musical instrument","a foreign language","sailing","pottery","computer programming","dancing","painting","cooking"]},{prompt:"For questions 26–30, choose from the list (A–H) what each speaker has gained from the experience.",options:["new friendships","more patience","a new source of income","greater self-confidence","a better relationship with a family member","a way of relaxing","a new career direction","a different view of failure"]}],speakers:[{a:[3,7],x:["D: ‘my bowls collapsed on the wheel every single time’.","H: ‘I used to be terrified of getting things wrong… now I actually enjoy the mistakes’."],script:[["F","I signed up for a pottery class on a whim. For the first six weeks, my bowls collapsed on the wheel every single time, and everyone else's looked perfect. I nearly quit. But the teacher kept saying, ‘Clay remembers everything – just start again.’ In my job, I used to be terrified of getting things wrong. Now I actually enjoy the mistakes, because that's where you learn something. It's changed the way I work, too."]]},{a:[1,4],x:["B: ‘I decided to learn Italian so I could talk to my father-in-law.’","E: ‘Now we chat for hours… we're closer than we've ever been.’"],script:[["M","My wife's father is Italian and speaks very little English, and for twenty years our conversations were basically smiling and nodding. So at forty-five I decided to learn Italian so I could actually talk to him. It was hard – my memory isn't what it was. But now we chat for hours on the phone about football and politics. We're closer than we've ever been, and he tells everyone his English son-in-law speaks like a Roman."]]},{a:[4,6],x:["E: ‘I did an online coding course in the evenings’.","G: ‘I'm starting a job as a junior developer next month.’"],script:[["F","I'd worked in a shop for years and I was bored, so I did an online coding course in the evenings, mainly out of curiosity. I found I was good at it – it's like solving puzzles all day. I made a couple of small apps for friends, and then a company saw one and asked if I'd like to apply. I'm starting a job as a junior developer next month. I'd never have imagined it."]]},{a:[5,0],x:["F: ‘salsa classes… I've got two left feet’.","A: ‘the people in that class are now some of my closest friends’."],script:[["M","After my divorce I was lonely, and my sister practically forced me to go to salsa classes. I've got two left feet, and I'm still not much of a dancer, to be honest. But that's not really the point. We go out for a meal after every class, we've been on holiday together – the people in that class are now some of my closest friends. I'd never have met them otherwise."]]},{a:[2,5],x:["C: ‘I took up sailing… on the lake near my house’.","F: ‘It's the only time my mind goes completely quiet.’"],script:[["F","I have a very stressful job in a hospital, and a colleague suggested I try sailing on the lake near my house. I thought it would be terrifying, but it's the opposite. Out there, you have to concentrate on the wind and the water, and there's no room for anything else. It's the only time my mind goes completely quiet. I go every Sunday, whatever the weather."]]}]}}});
